{
  "$comment": "Reviewed Evangelizo Syriac English (SYE) and Arabic (SYA) observance identities for 2026-07-01 through 2026-12-08. Exact Arabic source titles are matched by name and biography, never by date or array position. English wording reuses source/catalog identities where established; Arabic-only names receive faithful descriptive metadata translations. Published Arabic captions remain unchanged.",
  "sources": [
    "https://publication.evangelizo.ws/SYE/days/{date}",
    "https://publication.evangelizo.ws/SYA/days/{date}"
  ],
  "reviewNotes": [
    "2026-07-02: SYA biography explicitly identifies Bishoy of Scetis (c. 320-417). SYE Vichai, Hermit has no biography; uncertain transcription remains a separate identity.",
    "2026-07-14: SYA Justus is the Egyptian martyr, husband of Theoclia and father of Apali, sent to Antinoe; SYE explicitly names Justus of Beauvais. Kept distinct.",
    "2026-07-21: SYA biography explicitly names Simeon Salos, a sixth-century ascetic of Emesa feigning madness. SYE names Simeon Stylites, Confessor. Kept distinct; its liturgy aliases only its own English saint.",
    "2026-08-09: SYA biography identifies Matthias, selected to replace Judas; SYE says Matthew, Apostle. Kept distinct.",
    "2026-08-20: SYA names Labai (Jude) and describes the apostle Jude. SYE names Levi. Kept distinct.",
    "2026-08-28: SYA title calls Augustine a bishop of Carthage; the biography identifies Augustine, son of Monica, converted under Ambrose. Matched to SYE Augustine of Hippo by biography, not by the inconsistent episcopal place in its title.",
    "2026-08-29: SYA calls Flavianus Michael Malke blessed; SYE calls him saint. Retained source captions, with shared identity rather than changing either published status.",
    "2026-09-18 and 2026-11-16: plain SYE St. Matthew is reused for a hermit/abbot and an apostle. englishSaintIds scopes disambiguation to the source UUID and explicit description. The August 9 English Matthew remains Matthew and is not equated with the Arabic Matthias.",
    "2026-10-11 and 2026-11-14: plain SYE St. Philip denotes one of the seven deacons and the apostle respectively. UUID-scoped identities keep them distinct.",
    "2026-12-04: SYE groups Barbara and Elena; SYA names Barbara alone. Kept distinct rather than treating the Arabic singleton as a translation of the complete English group.",
    "2026-12-05: SYA biography identifies Sabas, born near Caesarea in Cappadocia in 439, the Palestinian monastic founder. SYE names Julian, Martyr. Kept distinct."
  ],
